1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,130
On a Saturday night in February 1944,

2
00:00:03,150 --> 00:00:05,217
in a snowstorm, a seventeen-year-old

3
00:00:05,237 --> 00:00:08,138
schoolteacher was alone in a farmhouse in

4
00:00:08,158 --> 00:00:09,807
Manitoba, in western Canada.

5
00:00:09,827 --> 00:00:13,145
At about half past ten, someone knocked on

6
00:00:13,165 --> 00:00:13,000
the door.

7
00:00:14,700 --> 00:00:17,043
Two young men stood in the snow.

8
00:00:17,063 --> 00:00:19,743
They wore blue denim, with a red stripe

9
00:00:19,763 --> 00:00:22,781
down each trouser leg and a big red circle

10
00:00:22,801 --> 00:00:24,807
on the back of the jacket.

11
00:00:24,827 --> 00:00:26,832
They were German prisoners of war.

12
00:00:26,852 --> 00:00:29,200
They said their names were Willi and

13
00:00:29,220 --> 00:00:31,376
Reinhard, and they asked to come in out of

14
00:00:31,396 --> 00:00:32,196
the storm.

15
00:00:33,125 --> 00:00:34,444
She let them in.

16
00:00:34,464 --> 00:00:37,458
They sat at the table and helped her with

17
00:00:37,478 --> 00:00:38,462
a jigsaw puzzle.

18
00:00:38,482 --> 00:00:41,141
Less than an hour later, the door opened

19
00:00:41,161 --> 00:00:43,505
again, and a Canadian officer walked in.

20
00:00:44,725 --> 00:00:46,792
This is the documented story of a prisoner

21
00:00:46,812 --> 00:00:48,879
of war camp in the Canadian forest that

22
00:00:48,899 --> 00:00:51,488
had no fence, of the men who walked out of

23
00:00:51,508 --> 00:00:53,575
it at night and came back by morning,

24
00:00:53,595 --> 00:00:55,923
and of the teacher who paid for one of

25
00:00:55,943 --> 00:00:56,743
those nights.

26
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:00,410
Most of the prisoners came from the Afrika

27
00:01:00,430 --> 00:01:03,260
Korps, the German army in North Africa.

28
00:01:03,280 --> 00:01:05,920
Thousands were captured in Egypt after the

29
00:01:05,940 --> 00:01:07,820
British victory at El Alamein,

30
00:01:07,840 --> 00:01:10,720
late in 1942.

31
00:01:10,740 --> 00:01:13,191
Britain had no room to hold them.

32
00:01:13,211 --> 00:01:15,309
Ships carried them across the Atlantic,

33
00:01:15,329 --> 00:01:17,800
and trains took them north into Canada.

34
00:01:19,100 --> 00:01:22,635
By the end of 1942, Canada held more than

35
00:01:22,655 --> 00:01:24,009
sixteen thousand German prisoners,

36
00:01:24,029 --> 00:01:27,102
most of them in two huge camps in Alberta,

37
00:01:27,122 --> 00:01:28,840
at Lethbridge and Medicine Hat.

38
00:01:30,125 --> 00:01:33,134
Medicine Hat was crowded, and hard Nazis

39
00:01:33,154 --> 00:01:34,432
ran the barracks.

40
00:01:34,452 --> 00:01:38,057
In 1943, a prisoner suspected of turning

41
00:01:38,077 --> 00:01:40,861
against Hitler was killed there by his own

42
00:01:40,881 --> 00:01:41,211
comrades.

43
00:01:41,231 --> 00:01:44,385
Whitewater would be a long way from that compound.

44
00:01:46,125 --> 00:01:49,972
In the winter of 1942, Canada ran short of

45
00:01:49,992 --> 00:01:50,358
firewood.

46
00:01:50,378 --> 00:01:53,452
Almost half the homes in the country were

47
00:01:53,472 --> 00:01:54,612
heated with wood.

48
00:01:54,632 --> 00:01:57,854
In May 1943, Ottawa decided that prisoners

49
00:01:57,874 --> 00:02:00,034
of war could be sent to work in the

50
00:02:00,054 --> 00:02:01,265
forests and on the farms.

51
00:02:02,675 --> 00:02:04,558
The government chose Riding Mountain

52
00:02:04,578 --> 00:02:06,460
National Park, in western Manitoba,

53
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:08,363
about three hundred kilometres northwest

54
00:02:08,383 --> 00:02:09,124
of Winnipeg.

55
00:02:09,144 --> 00:02:11,788
Old fires had left dead timber there,

56
00:02:11,808 --> 00:02:13,690
and the place was remote.

57
00:02:13,710 --> 00:02:17,115
The camp went up in the late summer of

58
00:02:17,135 --> 00:02:20,337
1943, on the northeast shore of a lake

59
00:02:20,357 --> 00:02:21,157
called Whitewater.

60
00:02:22,775 --> 00:02:25,032
It was a well built camp.

61
00:02:25,052 --> 00:02:26,930
There were about fifteen buildings:

62
00:02:26,950 --> 00:02:29,587
six large bunkhouses, each with its own

63
00:02:29,607 --> 00:02:32,244
washroom and showers, a kitchen and dining

64
00:02:32,264 --> 00:02:34,141
hall, a small hospital, barns,

65
00:02:34,161 --> 00:02:36,798
a machine shop, and a power plant.

66
00:02:36,818 --> 00:02:39,475
It cost about three hundred thousand dollars.

67
00:02:40,175 --> 00:02:42,444
It did not have a fence.

68
00:02:42,464 --> 00:02:45,878
There was no barbed wire and no guard tower.

69
00:02:45,898 --> 00:02:48,549
The boundary was a ring of trees,

70
00:02:48,569 --> 00:02:50,075
marked with red blazes.

71
00:02:50,095 --> 00:02:52,364
Beyond the trees there was forest,

72
00:02:52,384 --> 00:02:55,417
and beyond the forest, a continent at war

73
00:02:55,437 --> 00:02:56,180
with Germany.

74
00:02:56,200 --> 00:02:59,232
Some called it the only camp in North

75
00:02:59,252 --> 00:03:00,758
America without a fence.

76
00:03:00,778 --> 00:03:03,047
Historians who study Canada's bush camps

77
00:03:03,067 --> 00:03:04,975
say others had none either.

78
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:08,503
On October twenty-sixth, 1943,

79
00:03:08,523 --> 00:03:10,755
four hundred and forty prisoners arrived

80
00:03:10,775 --> 00:03:11,881
from Medicine Hat.

81
00:03:11,901 --> 00:03:14,133
According to the archaeologist who later

82
00:03:14,153 --> 00:03:17,136
dug at the site, they had volunteered to

83
00:03:17,156 --> 00:03:19,763
come, on the understanding that they would

84
00:03:19,783 --> 00:03:21,660
work hard once they arrived.

85
00:03:22,975 --> 00:03:24,692
The work was cutting firewood.

86
00:03:24,713 --> 00:03:27,472
The men worked in gangs, eight hours a

87
00:03:27,493 --> 00:03:29,210
day, six days a week.

88
00:03:29,230 --> 00:03:31,990
Each man was expected to cut and stack

89
00:03:32,010 --> 00:03:34,422
three quarters of a cord a day.

90
00:03:34,442 --> 00:03:36,875
The pay was fifty cents a day.

91
00:03:38,100 --> 00:03:40,010
Five days after they arrived,

92
00:03:40,030 --> 00:03:41,960
nineteen prisoners were found missing.

93
00:03:43,750 --> 00:03:46,417
The planners had counted on the forest.

94
00:03:46,437 --> 00:03:49,104
Many years later, one of the prisoners,

95
00:03:49,124 --> 00:03:51,024
Klaus Meyer, put it simply.

96
00:03:51,044 --> 00:03:52,943
Escape, he said, was impossible.

97
00:03:52,963 --> 00:03:55,650
So they made the best of it.

98
00:03:56,800 --> 00:03:59,180
The guards were men of the Veterans Guard

99
00:03:59,200 --> 00:04:01,580
of Canada, soldiers of the First World War

100
00:04:01,600 --> 00:04:03,680
who were too old to fight overseas.

101
00:04:03,700 --> 00:04:05,780
The camp trusted the prisoners with the

102
00:04:05,800 --> 00:04:07,880
power plant, the horses and the trucks,

103
00:04:07,900 --> 00:04:10,580
and some of them rode with the guards into

104
00:04:10,600 --> 00:04:13,040
the town of Dauphin to pick up supplies.

105
00:04:14,425 --> 00:04:16,658
The prisoners spent their wages through

106
00:04:16,678 --> 00:04:18,911
the Eaton's catalogue, the mail-order book

107
00:04:18,931 --> 00:04:21,164
that reached farms all over Canada.

108
00:04:21,184 --> 00:04:23,793
Once, they threatened to go on strike

109
00:04:23,813 --> 00:04:26,797
because the pyjamas they had ordered were late.

110
00:04:26,817 --> 00:04:28,299
They also ordered watches.

111
00:04:28,319 --> 00:04:31,303
In the forest, some of them turned those

112
00:04:31,323 --> 00:04:32,825
watches into crude compasses.

113
00:04:34,625 --> 00:04:36,892
South of the park lived farm families,

114
00:04:36,912 --> 00:04:39,178
many of them immigrants from Ukraine and

115
00:04:39,198 --> 00:04:41,138
Eastern Europe, in small places called

116
00:04:41,158 --> 00:04:43,098
Olha, Horod, Seech, and Crawford Park.

117
00:04:43,118 --> 00:04:45,385
At night, prisoners slipped out of the

118
00:04:45,405 --> 00:04:47,998
camp, walked to the edge of the park,

119
00:04:48,018 --> 00:04:50,305
and were met by farmers with sleighs.

120
00:04:51,400 --> 00:04:54,710
They went to the dances, where they were popular.

121
00:04:54,730 --> 00:04:56,930
They brought sugar, which was rationed,

122
00:04:56,950 --> 00:04:59,890
and which the farmers needed for their stills.

123
00:04:59,910 --> 00:05:01,740
Some farmers gave them clothes.

124
00:05:01,760 --> 00:05:03,590
Before the morning roll call,

125
00:05:03,610 --> 00:05:05,460
they were back in camp.

126
00:05:06,750 --> 00:05:09,581
If they were caught, the story was always

127
00:05:09,601 --> 00:05:10,294
the same.

128
00:05:10,314 --> 00:05:13,502
They had gone for a hike, and got lost.

129
00:05:13,522 --> 00:05:15,996
The officers did not treat them as

130
00:05:16,016 --> 00:05:18,135
escapees, because they always came back.

131
00:05:18,155 --> 00:05:20,630
And the Mounted Police would not get

132
00:05:20,650 --> 00:05:22,768
involved unless a prisoner was reported

133
00:05:22,788 --> 00:05:24,570
missing, or a civilian complained.

134
00:05:26,350 --> 00:05:28,198
Catherine Chastko was seventeen.

135
00:05:28,218 --> 00:05:31,468
During the war, rural Manitoba was short

136
00:05:31,488 --> 00:05:32,402
of teachers.

137
00:05:32,422 --> 00:05:36,218
In the summer of 1943, after finishing

138
00:05:36,238 --> 00:05:38,204
grade eleven, she took a six-week course

139
00:05:38,224 --> 00:05:40,759
at a college in Winnipeg, and was sent to

140
00:05:40,779 --> 00:05:42,413
teach at the Zaporoza School,

141
00:05:42,433 --> 00:05:44,817
about fifteen miles north of her family's

142
00:05:44,837 --> 00:05:46,210
home in Shoal Lake.

143
00:05:47,500 --> 00:05:49,267
She boarded with a farm family,

144
00:05:49,287 --> 00:05:52,712
Peter and Anne Chrun, and their two little

145
00:05:52,732 --> 00:05:53,160
daughters.

146
00:05:53,180 --> 00:05:54,504
She was lonely.

147
00:05:54,524 --> 00:05:58,673
Early in 1944, she went to a dance at Seech.

148
00:05:58,693 --> 00:06:01,040
That was where she first met the prisoners.

149
00:06:02,825 --> 00:06:05,845
On February nineteenth, 1944,

150
00:06:05,865 --> 00:06:08,419
the Chruns went to a wedding dance about

151
00:06:08,439 --> 00:06:09,546
two miles away.

152
00:06:09,566 --> 00:06:10,674
Catherine stayed home.

153
00:06:10,694 --> 00:06:12,176
It was snowing hard.

154
00:06:12,196 --> 00:06:15,182
At about half past ten, there was the

155
00:06:15,202 --> 00:06:16,685
knock at the door.

156
00:06:16,705 --> 00:06:19,525
Willi and Reinhard.

157
00:06:20,225 --> 00:06:22,567
That same night, the officer in charge of

158
00:06:22,587 --> 00:06:23,749
the guards, Lieutenant Mann,

159
00:06:23,769 --> 00:06:26,111
had taken a patrol out of the camp.

160
00:06:26,131 --> 00:06:28,473
Less than an hour after the two prisoners

161
00:06:28,493 --> 00:06:30,865
came in, he walked into the Chrun house

162
00:06:30,885 --> 00:06:32,545
and ordered them out.

163
00:06:32,565 --> 00:06:34,225
Catherine was in tears.

164
00:06:34,245 --> 00:06:37,605
She told him she had never invited them.

165
00:06:38,850 --> 00:06:41,140
Mann went on to the wedding dance.

166
00:06:41,160 --> 00:06:43,450
There was shouting and pushing between his

167
00:06:43,470 --> 00:06:46,090
men and the local men, among them Peter

168
00:06:46,110 --> 00:06:48,570
Chrun, who insisted the prisoners had

169
00:06:48,590 --> 00:06:50,290
never visited the teacher before.

170
00:06:50,310 --> 00:06:52,698
By the next morning, seven Germans had

171
00:06:52,718 --> 00:06:55,470
been rounded up and brought back to camp.

172
00:06:57,150 --> 00:06:58,707
Mann wrote a report.

173
00:06:58,727 --> 00:07:00,679
The prisoners' visit, he wrote,

174
00:07:00,699 --> 00:07:02,256
had evidently been pre-arranged.

175
00:07:02,276 --> 00:07:04,621
The teacher had admitted meeting them

176
00:07:04,641 --> 00:07:06,199
before, at a dance.

177
00:07:06,219 --> 00:07:08,959
He sent word to the provincial Department

178
00:07:08,979 --> 00:07:09,747
of Education.

179
00:07:09,767 --> 00:07:13,296
And on his way back, he stopped in Shoal

180
00:07:13,316 --> 00:07:16,470
Lake, to complain about her to her father.

181
00:07:18,225 --> 00:07:20,525
About two weeks later, Willi and Reinhard

182
00:07:20,545 --> 00:07:22,528
came back, at night, to apologize.

183
00:07:22,548 --> 00:07:24,866
They brought a ship in a bottle,

184
00:07:24,886 --> 00:07:27,203
and a wooden box they had made,

185
00:07:27,223 --> 00:07:28,539
full of chocolate bars.

186
00:07:28,559 --> 00:07:31,544
They told her that someone on the road had

187
00:07:31,564 --> 00:07:34,215
said she was alone, and that the same

188
00:07:34,235 --> 00:07:35,884
person had told the guards.

189
00:07:35,904 --> 00:07:37,888
Outside a window there were footprints.

190
00:07:37,908 --> 00:07:40,245
At least two people had been watching.

191
00:07:41,575 --> 00:07:44,108
She never saw Willi or Reinhard again.

192
00:07:44,128 --> 00:07:46,075
But the prisoners kept coming.

193
00:07:46,095 --> 00:07:49,222
In April, one of them stood watching her

194
00:07:49,242 --> 00:07:51,582
teach, noticed that the classroom clock

195
00:07:51,602 --> 00:07:53,548
was broken, and fixed it.

196
00:07:53,568 --> 00:07:56,302
Another sat on the school steps during

197
00:07:56,322 --> 00:07:57,895
recess, apologized, and left.

198
00:07:59,200 --> 00:08:01,785
At the end of the school year,

199
00:08:01,805 --> 00:08:03,274
Catherine Chastko went home.

200
00:08:03,294 --> 00:08:05,135
Her teaching days were over.

201
00:08:05,155 --> 00:08:07,368
That summer she went to Ontario,

202
00:08:07,388 --> 00:08:10,345
and worked on an assembly line in a

203
00:08:10,365 --> 00:08:11,090
munitions factory.

204
00:08:11,110 --> 00:08:13,695
The record does not show what the

205
00:08:13,715 --> 00:08:16,320
Department of Education did with Mann's report.

206
00:08:18,025 --> 00:08:20,094
Back at Whitewater, the prisoners had

207
00:08:20,114 --> 00:08:21,487
found another way out.

208
00:08:21,507 --> 00:08:23,228
With saws, axes and chisels,

209
00:08:23,248 --> 00:08:25,666
they carved dugout canoes from big spruce

210
00:08:25,686 --> 00:08:27,755
logs, twelve to sixteen feet long,

211
00:08:27,775 --> 00:08:30,541
even though the park warden asked them to

212
00:08:30,561 --> 00:08:31,585
spare the spruce.

213
00:08:31,605 --> 00:08:34,371
One account says the idea came from a

214
00:08:34,391 --> 00:08:36,460
Canadian magazine with a birch-bark canoe

215
00:08:36,480 --> 00:08:37,525
on the cover.

216
00:08:38,825 --> 00:08:41,167
The guards let them paddle on Whitewater

217
00:08:41,187 --> 00:08:44,203
Lake, as long as they stayed away from the

218
00:08:44,223 --> 00:08:46,565
shore and were back before roll call.

219
00:08:46,585 --> 00:08:47,915
One prisoner, Konstantin Schwartz,

220
00:08:47,935 --> 00:08:50,614
wrote in his diary about paddling out of

221
00:08:50,634 --> 00:08:52,301
the reeds onto open water.

222
00:08:52,321 --> 00:08:54,345
The lake, he wrote, was ours.

223
00:08:55,700 --> 00:08:58,032
Then prisoners at other camps drowned,

224
00:08:58,052 --> 00:08:59,992
and orders came from Ottawa.

225
00:09:00,012 --> 00:09:02,344
At Whitewater, canoeing was first limited,

226
00:09:02,364 --> 00:09:03,540
and then banned.

227
00:09:05,300 --> 00:09:06,854
There was a choir, sports,

228
00:09:06,874 --> 00:09:08,429
reading clubs, a camp radio,

229
00:09:08,449 --> 00:09:09,058
and theatre.

230
00:09:09,078 --> 00:09:11,892
The camp had a mascot, a black bear cub,

231
00:09:11,912 --> 00:09:14,096
which raided the kitchen and spent the

232
00:09:14,116 --> 00:09:16,320
winter asleep under one of the buildings.

233
00:09:17,700 --> 00:09:19,966
Not every day was easy.

234
00:09:19,986 --> 00:09:24,368
In March 1944, a prisoner was killed by a

235
00:09:24,388 --> 00:09:25,037
falling tree.

236
00:09:25,057 --> 00:09:28,820
The others went to his funeral in Dauphin.

237
00:09:28,840 --> 00:09:31,000
And not every prisoner was content.

238
00:09:31,020 --> 00:09:33,543
Some were ardent Nazis, and there was

239
00:09:33,563 --> 00:09:35,380
tension in the work gangs.

240
00:09:37,125 --> 00:09:40,753
In the middle of 1944, the camp was cut to

241
00:09:40,773 --> 00:09:41,959
about two hundred men.

242
00:09:41,979 --> 00:09:45,783
By the spring of 1945, the prisoners were

243
00:09:45,803 --> 00:09:48,530
bored with cutting wood, and slowed down

244
00:09:48,550 --> 00:09:49,315
on purpose.

245
00:09:49,335 --> 00:09:53,205
Woodcutting ended in March 1945.

246
00:09:53,225 --> 00:09:56,245
That summer, the last men were loaned to

247
00:09:56,265 --> 00:09:57,005
local farmers.

248
00:09:57,025 --> 00:09:58,925
In September, the camp closed.

249
00:10:00,275 --> 00:10:02,602
The buildings were advertised for sale in

250
00:10:02,622 --> 00:10:04,948
a Winnipeg newspaper, and a wrecking firm

251
00:10:04,968 --> 00:10:05,954
took them down.

252
00:10:05,974 --> 00:10:09,675
By the spring of 1946, they were gone.

253
00:10:09,695 --> 00:10:11,822
The Parks Bureau tried to erase every

254
00:10:11,842 --> 00:10:14,295
trace of a prison camp in the park.

255
00:10:16,000 --> 00:10:17,425
Klaus Meyer went home.

256
00:10:17,445 --> 00:10:19,593
Many years later, living near Cologne,

257
00:10:19,613 --> 00:10:22,483
he remembered the fresh air and the open

258
00:10:22,503 --> 00:10:25,013
spaces, and said the treatment had been

259
00:10:25,033 --> 00:10:25,735
very good.

260
00:10:25,755 --> 00:10:27,542
They were not die-hard Nazis,

261
00:10:27,562 --> 00:10:28,264
he said.

262
00:10:28,284 --> 00:10:30,793
They were young soldiers who got caught,

263
00:10:30,813 --> 00:10:32,620
and wanted to go home.

264
00:10:33,875 --> 00:10:35,115
Catherine Chastko married,

265
00:10:35,135 --> 00:10:36,795
and became Catherine Dobrowski.

266
00:10:36,815 --> 00:10:40,155
Decades later, she read a book by the

267
00:10:40,175 --> 00:10:42,416
historian Bill Waiser that described the

268
00:10:42,436 --> 00:10:45,246
raid on the farmhouse, and she phoned him,

269
00:10:45,266 --> 00:10:47,368
upset by how it was told.

270
00:10:47,388 --> 00:10:50,295
In 2009, Waiser published her side of the story.

271
00:10:51,625 --> 00:10:53,804
Between 2009 and 2011, archaeologists dug

272
00:10:53,824 --> 00:10:54,537
at Whitewater.

273
00:10:54,557 --> 00:10:56,369
They found Red Cross tins,

274
00:10:56,389 --> 00:10:57,835
buttons, letters, and photographs,

